Bilinear maps and related assumptions. Let G, GT be multiplicative
groups of prime order p

with an admissible map ˆe : G × G →GT. By admissible we mean that the
map is bilinear, non-

degenerate and efficiently computable. Bilinearity means that for all a,
b ∈Zp and all g ∈G we have

e(ga, gb) = ˆe(g, g)ab. By non-degenerate we mean that ˆe(g, g) = 1 if
and only if g = 1.

\begin{quote}
In such a setting we can define a number of computational problems. The
first we shall be
\end{quote}

