\begin{quote}
and by using his secret key to decrypt the ciphertext C′= (C1, C′2, C3)
via the Dec algorithm of the \emph{Wa}-\emph{HIBE} scheme.
\end{quote}

The master public key of the \emph{Wa}-\emph{WIBE} scheme contains (n +
1)L + 3 group elements. Encrypting to a pattern of length ℓcontaining w
wildcards comes at the cost of ℓ+ nw + 2 exponentiations andℓ+ nw + 2
group elements in the ciphertext; in the worst case of ℓ= w = L this
means (n + 1)L + 2 exponentiations and group elements. (The pairing
ˆe(h1, g2) can be precomputed.) Decryption requires the computation of
ℓ+ 1 pairings.

In terms of efficiency, the \emph{Wa}-\emph{WIBE} scheme performs well
enough to be considered for use in practice, but definitely leaves room
for improvement. The main problem is the dependency of the scheme on n,
the bit length of identity strings. In practice, one would typically use
the output of a collision-resistant hash function as identity strings,
so that n = 160 for a reasonable level of security. We note that the
techniques of {[}CS06, Nac05{]} could be applied to trade a factor d of
efficiency against losing a factor 2Ldin the tightness of the reduction.

We now prove the security of the \emph{Wa}-\emph{WIBE} scheme. To make
the proof more modular, and to avoid repeating the work of {[}Wat05{]},
we do this by reducing to the security of the \emph{Wa}-\emph{HIBE}
scheme, rather than to the BDDH problem directly.

Theorem 5.1 If the \emph{Wa}-\emph{HIBE} of depth L is (t, qK, ǫ)
IND-ID-CPA-secure, then the \emph{Wa}-\emph{WIBE} scheme of depth L is
(t′, q′K, ǫ′) IND-WID-CPA-secure for all

