We will also use this encoding function with two arguments to denote

Encode(ID, vk) = (0, ID1, . . . , 0, ID k, 1, vk) .

Given a WIBE scheme \emph{WIBE} = (Setup, KeyDer, Enc, Dec) of depth 2L
+ 2 with identity space \emph{ID}≤L,

consider the following WIBE scheme \emph{WIBE}′= (Setup, KeyDer′, Enc′,
Dec′) of depth L.

Key Derivation. The secret key of identity ID = (ID1, . . . , IDℓ) under
\emph{WIBE}′is the secret key

\begin{quote}
corresponding to identity Encode(ID) = (0, ID1, . . . , 0, ID ℓ) under
\emph{WIBE}.
\end{quote}

Encryption. To encrypt a message m under a pattern P and using a master
public key mpk, the

\$

\begin{quote}
following steps are performed: First, we generate a signature key pair
(sk, vk)←SigGen. Then

\$ \$

we compute C←Enc(mpk, Encode(P, vk), m) and σ←Sign(sk, C). The final
ciphertext is the

tuple (vk, C, σ).
\end{quote}

Decryption. To decrypt a ciphertext (vk, C, σ) using a private key dID
for an identity ID, first check

\begin{quote}
that Verify(vk, C, σ) = valid. If not, output ⊥. Otherwise, compute d =
KeyDer(dID, (1, vk))

and output Dec(d, C). Note that in this case d is the decryption for the
identity Encode(ID, vk)

in \emph{WIBE}.
\end{quote}

Theorem 7.3 If \emph{WIBE} is (t, qK, ǫ) IND-WID-CPA-secure and (SigGen,
Sign, Verify) is (ts, ǫs) strongly

one-time secure, then \emph{WIBE}′is (t′, q′K, q′D, ǫ′)
