The main difference between the original HIBE scheme of {[}BB04{]} and
our variant above is that our scheme uses a different value ui,1 for
each level, while the original scheme uses the same value u1 for all
levels. Adding wildcard functionality to the original scheme would
require us to include ut 1in the ciphertext, but this ruins security as
it can be used to change the recipient identity for non-wildcard levels
as well.

Theorem A.1 If the (t, ǫ) BDDH assumption holds in G, then the
\emph{BB}-\emph{HIBE} scheme with hierarchy depth L is (t′, qK, ǫ)
IND-sID-CPA-secure, where t′= t −Θ(L · qK · texp) and texp is the
maximum time for an exponentiation in G.

Proof: The present proof follows very closely the proof of security for
the original scheme in {[}BB04{]}. As before, we assume that there exist
an adversary A that breaks the IND-sID-CPA-security of the HIBE scheme
\emph{BB}-\emph{HIBE} and then we show how to efficiently build another
adversary B that, using A as a black box, manages to solve the BDDH
problem in G.

Algorithm B first receives as input a random tuple (g, A = ga, B = gb, C
= gc, Z) and its goal is to determine whether Z = ˆe(g, g)abcor ˆe(g,
g)zfor a random element z in Zp. Algorithm B should output 1 if Z =
ˆe(g, g)abcand 0, otherwise. Algorithm B works as follows.

Initialisation. Algorithm B starts interacting with A in the IND-sID-CPA
game. Let ID∗= (ID∗1, . . . , ID∗ℓ∗), where 0 ≤ℓ∗≤L be the challenge
identity outputted by A. If necessary, B appends random elements in Zp
to ID∗so that ID∗is a vector of length L.

Setup. To generate the systems parameters, B first sets g1 ←g, h1 ←A,
and g2 ←B. Algorithm B then chooses α1,0, . . . , αL,0, α1,1, . . . ,
αL,1←Z∗pat random and sets ui,0←gαi,0· h−ID∗1 iαi,1 and ui,1 ←hαi,1 for
i = 1, . . . , L. Next, B gives to A as the master public key the value
mpk ←(g1, h1, g2, u1,0, . . . , uL,0, u1,1, . . . , uL,1). Note that the
corresponding master secret key msk = ga 2is unknown to B.

Key Derivation queries. During the phases of its attack against the
IND-sID-CPA-security of \emph{BB}-\emph{HIBE}, A can query up to qK
queries to its key derivation oracle. Let ID = (ID1, . . . , IDℓ), where
IDi ∈Zp and ℓ≤L, be one such query. Thus, ID cannot be a prefix of ID∗.
Let j be

