where r1, . . . , rj−1, ˜rj are uniformly distributed over Zp. From (a0,
a1, . . . , aj), algorithm B can

derive the key for ID as in the key derivation algorithm.
\end{quote}

Challenge. Let (m∗0, m∗1) be the pair of messages that A outputs at the
end of the first phase of the

\begin{quote}
IND-sID-CPA game. Algorithm B then chooses a random bit b ∈\{0, 1\} and
sends C∗= (C,

m∗b· Z, Cα1,0, . . . , Cαℓ∗,0) to A as the challenge ciphertext. Since
ui,0 · uID∗= gαi,0 1 for all i, we

have that
\end{quote}

ID∗

\begin{quote}
C∗= (gc 1, m∗b· Z, (u1,0 · uID∗ℓ∗,1)c).

As a result, when Z = ˆe(g, g)abc= ˆe(h1, g2)c, C∗is a valid encryption
of message m∗bfor the

challenge identity ID∗= (ID∗1, . . . , ID∗ℓ∗). On the other hand, when Z
= ˆe(g, g)zfor a random

value z ∈Zp, then the challenge ciphertext is independent of b from the
view point of the ad-

versary.
\end{quote}

Guess. Let b′be the output of A at the end of the second phase of the
IND-sID-CPA game. If b = b′,

\begin{quote}
then algorithm B outputs 1, guessing that Z = ˆe(g, g)abc. Otherwise, B
outputs 0.
\end{quote}

Clearly, when Z = ˆe(g, g)abc, the view of A is identical to its view in
a real attack and, thus, the

probability that b = b′is exactly the probability that A wins the
IND-sID-CPA game. On the other

hand, when Z is a random group element in GT, then the probability that
b = b′is exactly 1/2. From

the above, the result announced in Theorem A.1 follows immediately.

24

B The \emph{BBG}-\emph{HIBE} Scheme

In this section we present the HIBE scheme due to Boneh, Boyen and Goh
{[}BBG05{]}, referred to as the \emph{BBG}-\emph{HIBE} scheme here. The
Setup and KeyDer algorithms are exactly as in the \emph{BBG}-\emph{WIBE}
scheme presented in Section 6.2. Encryption and decryption work as
follows.

Encryption. To encrypt a message m ∈GT for an identity ID = (ID1, . . .
, IDℓ), the sender first

